45: \nL46: [Select]\nL47: \nL48: Modifying this control will update this page automatically\nL49: \nL50: cite21†Table of Contents L51: ## Japanese Input Method User Guide\nL52:   * cite14†Welcome L53:   * Use a Japanese input source\nL54:     * cite22†Set up the input source L55:     * cite23†Switch the input source L56:     * cite24†Edit and use your user dictionaries L57:     * Enter characters\nL58:       * cite25†Enter Japanese text L59:       * cite26†Enter characters using the Kana Table L60:       * cite27†Enter full-width or half-width numbers L61:       * cite28†Enter symbols L62:       * cite29†Change characters you entered L63:       * cite30†Use the candidate window L64:       * cite31†Type roman strings using hiragana or katakana L65:       * cite32†Roman characters and corresponding kana L66:     * Convert characters\nL67:       * cite33†Improve conversion accuracy L68:       * cite34†Convert hiragana numbers into other notations L69:       * cite35†Convert text using a punctuation mark L70:       * cite36†Insert and convert characters between characters L71:       * cite37†Reverse a conversion L72:       * cite38†Keyboard shortcuts L73:     * cite39†Change Japanese settings L74:   * Use the Ainu input source\nL75:     * cite40†Set up and switch to the Ainu input source L76:     * cite41†Look up roman/kana character correlation L77:   * cite42†Choose a sort order for lists in Japanese L78:   * cite43†Copyright and trademarks L79: cite44†Image†help.apple.com L80: # Switch to a Japanese input source on Mac\nL81: \nL82: After you cite45†set up the input sources you want to use on your Mac, you can easily switch between them.\nL83: ## Switch to a Japanese input source\nL84: \nL85: On your Mac, do any of the following:\nL86: \nL87:   * Use the Input menu: Click the cite46†Input menu in the menu bar, then choose an cite47†input source . If the input source is dimmed, the current app doesn’t support it.\nL88: You can also press Control-Option-Space bar to select the next input source in the Input menu, or Control-Space bar to select the previous input source. Or, to view all your input sources (displayed close to where you’re typing as characters or letters that represent each source), hold the Control key and press the Space bar. Continue pressing the Space bar until the input source you want to switch to is selected.\nL89:   * Use the Caps Lock key: You can set an option in Input Sources settings to change between Latin and non-Latin input sources by using the Caps Lock key. Choose Apple menu cite48†Image†help.apple.com > System Settings, then click Keyboard cite49†Image†help.apple.com in the sidebar (you may need to scroll down). Go to Text Input on the right, click Edit, then turn on “Use the Caps Lock key to switch to and from [last used Latin input source].”\nL90: \nL91: Open Keyboard settings for me\nL92: Press the Caps Lock key to switch between a non-Latin input source (such as Japanese) and a Latin input source (such as English).\nL93:   * Use the Fn key or the Globe key cite50†Image†help.apple.com (if available on the keyboard): You can set an option in Keyboard settings to change input sources by using the Fn key or the Globe key. Choose Apple menu > System Settings, then click Keyboard in the sidebar (you may need to scroll down). Click the “Press fn key to” or “Press cite50†Image: Globe†help.apple.com key to” pop-up menu, then choose Change Input Source.\nL94: \nL95: Open Keyboard settings for me\nL96: Press the Fn key or the Globe key to switch to the next input source and show all your input sources close to where you’re typing (displayed as characters or letters that represent each source). Continue pressing the key until the input source you want to switch to is selected.\nL97: ## Switch between Japanese input modes\nL98: \nL99: On your Mac, do one of the following:\nL100: \nL101:   * Switch to Hiragana: Do any of the following:\nL102: \nL103:     * On a keyboard: Press Control-Shift-J or Option-Shift-Z.\nL104: \nL105:     * On a JIS keyboard: Press the Kana key (かな).\nL106: \nL107:   * Switch to Romaji: Do any of the following:\nL108: \nL109:     * On a keyboard: Press Control-Shift-Apostrophe ('). The Romaji input mode must be cite51†selected in settings for the Japanese input source .\nL110: Note: You can also press Shift if the “Shift key action” option is set to “Enter Romaji mode.”\nL111: \nL112: To select the option, click the cite46†Input menu in the menu bar, choose Open Japanese - Kana Settings or Open Japanese - Romaji Settings, then click Japanese - Kana or Japanese - Romaji in the sidebar.\nL113: \nL114: Open Keyboard settings for me\nL115: \nL116:     * On a JIS keyboard: Press Control-Shift-Semicolon (;) or press the Eisu key (英数).\nL117:   * Switch to Full-width Romaji: Press Control-Shift-L. The Full-width Romaji input mode must be selected in settings for the Japanese input source.\nL118: \nL119:   * Switch to Katakana: Do any of the following:\nL120: \nL121:     * On a keyboard: Press Control-Shift-K or Option-Shift-X. The Katakana input mode must be selected in settings for the Japanese input source.\nL122: \nL123:     * On a JIS keyboard: Press Shift-Kana key (かな).\nL124: \nL125: Note: You can also press Shift if the “Shift key action” option is set to Type Katakana.\nL126: To select the option, click the Input menu in the menu bar, choose Open Japanese - Kana Settings or Open Japanese - Romaji Settings, then click Japanese - Kana or Japanese - Romaji in the sidebar.\nL127: \nL128:   * Switch to Half-width Katakana: Press Control-Shift-Semicolon (;). The Half-width Katakana input mode must be selected in settings for the Japanese input source.\nL129: \nL130: See alsocite52†Enter Japanese text using a Japanese input source on Mac cite53†Enter symbols using a Japanese input source on Mac L131: \nL132: Helpful?\nL133: [Button: Yes ] [Button: No ]\nL134: \nL135: [Input]\nL136: \nL137: 250\nL138: \nL139: Please don’t include any personal information in your comment.\nL140: \nL141: Maximum character limit is 250.\nL142: \nL143: [Button: Submit ]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEnter Japanese text using a Japanese input source on Mac – Apple Support (UK) (https://support.apple.com/en-gb/guide/japanese-input-method/jpim10265/mac)\nciteturn10view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn9view0\",\"id\":28}); Total lines: 152\nL0: cite0†Image L1: \nL2:   * cite1†Apple†www.apple.com L3:   *     * cite2†Store†www.apple.com L4: \nL5:     * cite3†Mac†www.apple.com L6: \nL7:     * cite4†iPad†www.apple.com L8: \nL9:     * cite5†iPhone †www.apple.com L10: \nL11:     * cite6†Watch†www.apple.com L12: \nL13:     * cite7†Vision†www.apple.com L14: \nL15:     * cite8†AirPods†www.apple.com L16: \nL17:     * cite9†TV & Home†www.apple.com L18: \nL19:     * cite10†Entertainment†www.apple.com L20: \nL21:     * cite11†Accessories†www.apple.com L22: \nL23:     * cite12†Support L24: \nL25:   * [Input: Search apple.com][Input][Input][Input][Input]\nL26: \nL27:   * cite13†0+†www.apple.com L28: \nL29: [Input]\nL30: cite14†Japanese Input Method User Guide L31: \nL32: cite15†macOS Tahoe 26 L33: \nL34: cite16†macOS Sequoia 15 L35: \nL36: cite17†macOS Sonoma 14 L37: \nL38: cite18†macOS Ventura 13 L39: \nL40: cite19†macOS Monterey 12 L41: \nL42: cite20†macOS Big Sur 11.0 L43: \nL44: cite21†macOS Catalina 10.15 L45: \nL46: cite22†macOS Mojave 10.14 L47: \nL48: cite23†macOS High Sierra L49: \nL50: Select version:\nL51: \nL52: [Select]\nL53: \nL54: Modifying this control will update this page automatically\nL55: \nL56: cite24†Table of Contents L57: ## Japanese Input Method User Guide\nL58:   * cite14†Welcome L59:   * Use a Japanese input source\nL60:     * cite25†Set up the input source L61:     * cite26†Switch the input source L62:     * cite27†Edit and use your user dictionaries L63:     * Enter characters\nL64:       * cite28†Enter Japanese text L65:       * cite29†Enter characters using the Kana Table L66:       * cite30†Enter full-width or half-width numbers L67:       * cite31†Enter symbols L68:       * cite32†Change characters you entered L69:       * cite33†Use the candidate window L70:       * cite34†Type roman strings using hiragana or katakana L71:       * cite35†Roman characters and corresponding kana L72:     * Convert characters\nL73:       * cite36†Improve conversion accuracy L74:       * cite37†Convert hiragana numbers into other notations L75:       * cite38†Convert text using a punctuation mark L76:       * cite39†Insert and convert characters between characters L77:       * cite40†Reverse a conversion L78:       * cite41†Keyboard shortcuts L79:     * cite42†Change Japanese settings L80:   * Use the Ainu input source\nL81:     * cite43†Set up and switch to the Ainu input source L82:     * cite44†Look up roman/kana character correlation L83:   * cite45†Choose a sort order for lists in Japanese L84:   * cite46†Copyright and trademarks L85: cite47†Image†help.apple.com L86: # Enter Japanese text using a Japanese input source on Mac\nL87: \nL88: After you cite48†set up a Japanese input source , there are two ways you can enter Japanese text: Live Conversion and traditional conversion.\nL89: ## Use Live Conversion\nL90: \nL91: Live Conversion automatically converts hiragana characters into relevant Japanese text, including kanji, as you type. This eliminates the need to press the Space bar for individual word conversions.\nL92: \nL93:   1. On your Mac, cite49†switch to a Japanese input source .\nL94: \nL95:   2. Click the cite50†Input menu in the menu bar, then do one of the following:\nL96: \nL97:      * Choose Live Conversion (so that a checkmark appears next to it in the menu).\nL98:      * Choose Open Japanese - Kana Settings or Open Japanese - Romaji Settings, click Japanese - Kana or Japanese - Romaji in the sidebar, then select Live Conversion.\nL99: \nL100: Open Keyboard settings for me\nL101: \nL102:   3. In an app, type the hiragana characters. You can:\nL103: \nL104:      * Type the entire word so that all possible conversions appear.\nL105: \nL106:      * Press the Space bar one or more times to show additional candidates. To confirm the candidate, press the Return key.\nL107:      * Press the Escape key to recover the hiragana characters you typed with the conversion underline.\nL108: \nL109: If characters aren’t displaying as you expect, make sure that the font your app uses matches the cite51†font used in the candidate window , or turn oﬀ Live Conversion and cite52†use traditional conversion instead.\nL110: ## Use traditional conversion\nL111: \nL112: When you use traditional conversion, type the text in hiragana, then convert it to kanji.\nL113: \nL114:   1. On your Mac, cite49†switch to a Japanese input source (choose Hiragana).\nL115: \nL116:   2. Click the cite50†Input menu in the menu bar, choose Open Japanese - Kana Settings or Open Japanese - Romaji Settings, click Japanese - Kana or Japanese - Romaji in the sidebar, then deselect Live Conversion.\nL117: \nL118: Open Keyboard settings for me\nL119: \nL120: Important: This turns Live Conversion off.\nL121:   3. To enter hiragana characters in an app, do one of the following:\nL122: \nL123:      * Convert the characters to the recommended candidate: Press the Space bar.\nL124: \nL125:      * Choose a different conversion candidate: Press the Space bar until the cite53†candidate window opens, then select a candidate.\nL126: \nL127:   4. To confirm the candidate, press the Return key.\nL128: ## Use autocorrection\nL129: \nL130:   1. On your Mac, cite49†switch to a Japanese input source .\nL131: \n"}],"internal